\section{Hand a Conservative Answer to a Client Pass}

\begin{verbatim}
points_to, contents := solve_pointer_rules(snapshot)
for memory_read in snapshot.loads:
    stores := all_stores_with_intersecting_targets(memory_read)
    include_unknown_effects(stores)     # calls, escapes, missing models
    pass_candidates_to_cfg_solver(stores)
    # The CFG solver decides which candidates can reach this read.
\end{verbatim}

Add \verb|copy(r,q).| to model another assignment to \(r\). Recompute: \verb|pt(r,right)| and \verb|heap(right,right)| are new, and \verb|may_alias(r,q)| now holds; \verb|pt(s,right)| still holds. The flow-insensitive analysis treats assignments as alternative contributors regardless of their order. Deleting the added assignment removes the new facts, which means an incremental client must not use the old snapshot's store candidates as if they were current. Conversely, a candidate absent from a sound current result can be excluded from the client pass. Adams and colleagues describe a related staging choice, using a flow-insensitive value-flow representation to reduce the work of later, more precise dataflow analysis. The toy rule system here illustrates the conservative prefilter, not their particular pointer algorithm or an implementation claim about its solver.

Such a relation is an input to a compiler optimization, not a proof that two concrete runtime locations alias. A client may safely omit stores ruled out by a sound current result, but it needs its own flow, context, and effect reasoning before transforming a retained candidate.
